Add this line to the TOP of \`/src/styles/index.css\` (before any other imports):

\`\`\`css
@import "<PACKAGE_NAME>/style.css";
\`\`\`

This import defines all CSS custom properties (color tokens, spacing tokens,
radius tokens) used by this design system. Without it, var(--token-name)
references will resolve to nothing and the UI will have no styling.

## Step 2: Read the full design system stylesheet

After importing, read the full contents of the stylesheet to understand what
tokens are available:

\`\`\`
cat node_modules/<PACKAGE_NAME>/dist/style.css
\`\`\`

Do NOT use \`head -100\` or any truncated read. The file begins with Tailwind
preflight/reset CSS (box-sizing, margins, etc.) which is NOT the design system.
The actual token definitions (CSS custom properties like \`--text-default\`,
\`--bg-brand\`, \`--spacer-*\`, etc.) appear AFTER the preflight section.

Scan the full output for \`:root\` or \`@theme\` blocks containing \`--\` custom
property definitions. These are the tokens you must use in generated code.

## Step 3: Discover exported components

Before writing any custom UI, find out what the kit already provides.

1. Check the \`exports\` field in \`node_modules/<PACKAGE_NAME>/package.json\` to
   see which entry points are importable.
2. Read the entrypoint to enumerate exported names:

   \`\`\`
   cat node_modules/<PACKAGE_NAME>/dist/index.js
   \`\`\`

3. For each component you might use, read its source under
   \`node_modules/<PACKAGE_NAME>/dist/\` (or the bundled type definitions in
   \`dist/index.d.ts\`) to learn its props, accepted children, className
   overrides, and any hardcoded behavior.

Do NOT rely on \`/guidelines/components.md\` alone \u2014 if it is empty or
boilerplate, the entrypoint and component source are the source of truth.
`;var tT=n(458226),tE=n(743543);let tI="{{SETUP_DIFF}}",tC=`Your job is to examine the project setup and produce a setup guideline document that an AI code-generation agent can later use to correctly configure new projects to use this design system.

## Input

${tI}

The npm package list and agent constraints have been pre-written to \`/guidelines/setup.md\`. Read that file to see which packages to analyze.

## IMPORTANT: Output location
Read the existing \`/guidelines/setup.md\` file first, then APPEND your findings below the existing content. Do NOT remove or modify the pre-written sections. Do NOT write any other files. Do NOT print the guidelines to chat \u{2014} write them as a file.

## What to document in \`/guidelines/setup.md\`

- **Install instructions**: the exact install instructions with all required packages (peer dependencies included).
- **Required CSS imports**: list every CSS file that must be imported and the recommended import order.
- **Provider / wrapper components**: if the design system requires a provider component at the app root, document it with all required props and show the recommended entry point pattern.
- **Build tool configuration**: any required Vite/Webpack/PostCSS/Tailwind configuration.
- **TypeScript**: any \`tsconfig.json\` requirements.
- **Peer dependencies**: list all peer dependencies and their version constraints.
- **Common gotchas**: things that silently break if misconfigured.

## Process

For each installed package, find and read the most informative files. Prioritize:

1. **README / CHANGELOG** \u{2014} setup instructions, provider requirements, and usage examples.
2. **package.json** \u{2014} check \`peerDependencies\`, \`exports\` (entry points), and \`style\`/\`css\` fields (required CSS imports).
3. **Type definitions** (\`.d.ts\` files) \u{2014} provider components, configuration types.
4. **CSS/SCSS source files** \u{2014} required CSS imports, theme files.

Do NOT rely on external documentation websites. Everything you need must come from what is shipped in the packages.

## Formatting rules

- Use Markdown for all output.
- Use fenced code blocks with language tags for all code examples.
- Keep the document concise but complete \u{2014} target 100-300 lines.
- Do not invent information. If a token or prop doesn't exist in the installed packages, omit it.
- Order content from most-used to least-used.`,tj=["dependencies","devDependencies","peerDependencies"];function tN(e,t){let n={};try{n=JSON.parse(e)}catch{}let i=new Set(t),r={};for(let e of tj){let t=n[e];if(t&&"object"==typeof t)for(let[n,a]of Object.entries(t))i.has(n)&&(r[e]??={},r[e][n]=a)}return r}function tA(e){return`## Package Installation

**CRITICAL**: You MUST explicitly install every package listed below as a direct dependency, exactly as written.

Rules:
- Even if a package is already available as a transitive dependency (i.e., installed by another package), you MUST still add it as a direct dependency. Transitive availability does NOT count as installed.
- Use the **exact** package name shown. Packages with similar names (e.g., \`@scope-a/foo\` vs \`@scope-b/foo\`, or \`foo\` vs \`foo-core\`) are **different packages** and are NOT interchangeable. Never substitute one for another.
- This applies to your \`import\` statements too: import from these exact package names. Do NOT rewrite the scope to match the package this guideline ships inside, or any other scope you see in \`node_modules/\`. The packages below are upstream npm packages \u{2014} they live under their own names, regardless of where this guideline file is located.
- Do not skip any package. Do not reorder, rename, or omit any entry.

Add the following to your \`package.json\` dependencies:

\`\`\`json
